Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Summary:", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"efa6045d-ed31-48df-ae09-42c383113cf1"],

Cell["\<\
The above example has a number of implications for the modeling process.  It \
is a characteristic of higher dimensional ODE systems to display a rich range \
of behavior.  This can be both good and bad when ODEs and PDEs are used to \
model a physical process. Consider the following possibilities:

\t If the physical process is extremely stable, then it is important to \
insure that the mathematical system that represents the process also has the \
same degree of stability.

\t If the physical process displays limit cycles either part or all of the \
time, then it is important that the mathematical model properly represents \
this behavior.

\t If the physical process is chaotic in nature, then it is also possible to \
capture this behavior with a mathematical system.

In conclusion, a detailed understanding of both the mathematical and physical \
system is required to insure the creation of a successful model.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"51a0e180-4fc8-4872-aa99-b1d2395ebcb8"]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]]
